<section xml:id="HW11" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Homework 11</title>

  <p><alert> Due Thursday, April 23</alert></p>

  <p>
   We will denote by <m>\mathbb F_q</m> a field with <m>q</m> elements. 
  </p>


<exercises xml:id="hw11-ex">

    <exercise>
        <p>
            From the book: Chapter 15: 7.1, 7.7
        </p>
    </exercise>



  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
       [Problem A] 
       Let <m>K</m> be a field of characteristic <m>p</m>, not necessarily finite. Let <m>f(x)\in K[x]</m> be an irreducible polynomial which admits multiple roots in some field extension of <m>K</m>. 
       Show that there exists an irreducible polynomial <m>g(x)\in K[x]</m> such that <m>f(x)=g(x^{p})</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
        <p>
           What can you say about <m>f'</m>? 
        </p>
    </hint>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
      <p>
       [Problem B] Let <m>K</m> be a field of characteristic <m>p</m> such that <m>\varphi\colon K\to K</m> given by <m>\varphi(x)=x^{p}</m> is an isomorphism. Let <m>f(x)\in K[x]</m> be an irreducible polynomial. 
       Show that <m>f</m> has no multiple roots in any field extension of <m>K</m>.
      </p>
    </statement> 
    <hint>
        <p>
            Assume <m>f</m> has multiple roots. Use the problem above to get a contradiction 
            with the fact that <m>f</m> is irreducible: Try to show that <m>f=h^{p}</m> for some <m>h\in K[x]</m>.
        </p>
    </hint>
</exercise>



<exercise>
<statement>
      <p>
       [Problem C] Let <m>K</m> be a finite field with <m>q=p^{n}</m> elements (<m>p</m> is prime). 
       For every <m>y\in K</m>, prove that there exists a <em>unique</em> <m>x\in K</m> such that <m>y=x^{p}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement> 
    <hint>
        <p>
          Show that <m>f\colon K\to K</m> given by <m>f(x)=x^{p}</m> is an isomorphism. 
        </p>
    </hint>
</exercise>


<exercise>
<statement>
      <p>
       [Problem D]  Let <m>K</m> be a field with <m>p^{n}</m> elements, where <m>p</m> is an <em>odd</em> prime number. Write <m>p^{n}=2k+1</m> with <m>k\in \mathbb{N}</m>. 
       If <m>a\in K^{\times}</m>, show that the equation <m>x^{2}=a</m> has a solution if and only if <m>a^{k}=1</m>.
      </p>
    </statement> 
    <hint>
        <p>
          Recall that <m>K^{\times}</m> is cyclic.
        </p>
    </hint>
</exercise>

          </exercises>

        </section>   